Object-Oriented Programming Concepts Used are objects, encapsulation, abstraction, inheritance and polymorphism

 Classes and Objects
For classes we used CultivationAdventureApp, StoryPage, and StoryBrain as class that acts as a blueprint, while an object is an instance of that class.
For example, `StoryBrain storyBrain = StoryBrain();` creates an object that manages the story flow of the game.

 Encapsulation
We used encapsulation logic to separate the `StoryBrain` class instead of placing everything directly in the UI code.
This keeps the story data, choices, scene changes, and ending logic organized in one place.
The `StoryPage` only calls methods such as `getStoryText()`, `getChoices()`, `nextScene()`, and `restart()` without needing to directly manage all story details.

Abstraction
The program hides complex story navigation behind simple method calls using Abstraction.
For example, when the player selects a choice,
the UI only calls `storyBrain.nextScene(index)`. The internal logic of which scene comes next is handled inside `StoryBrain`.

Inheritance
The Flutter widgets demonstrate inheritance. `CultivationAdventureApp` extends `StatelessWidget`, while `StoryPage` extends `StatefulWidget`.
This means these classes inherit Flutter widget behavior and customize it by overriding the `build()` method.

Polymorphism
Polymorphism is shown through method overriding.
The `build()` method is overridden in both `CultivationAdventureApp` and `_StoryPageState` to provide different user interfaces while following Flutter’s widget structure.

the OOP concept helped us make the project organized, reusable, and easier to maintain by separating the user interface, story logic, and audio functionality into different classes and methods.
